description:
CATEGORY A data-correctness bug. The scholarly breakdown elements (toponym_etymology_element) are ordered by 'ordinal', but the ordinal reflects the SCHOLAR'S GLOSS / HEAD-FIRST (semantic) order, NOT the modern place-name SURFACE (left-to-right) order. Found while building wyrd-aicu.9: every Weston is stored [tūn(ord1), west(ord2)] but the surface is west+tūn ('Wes-ton'); so max(ordinal) yields the gloss-head ('west'), not the surface-final morpheme (tūn). It is systematic head-first (and may differ across sources, i.e. effectively unreliable corpus-wide), not random.

WHY THIS MATTERS (the trigger): the scholarly breakdowns ARE the labeled GROUND-TRUTH TEST CORPUS for the decomposer (a decomposer can be graded by running it over every labeled toponym and comparing morphemes to the scholar's breakdown — see wyrd-aicu.9 grading). With non-surface ordinals:
- the grader can only do order-robust SET agreement, not the stronger exact-SEQUENCE agreement (the real bar for a decomposer);
- last/head-element detection via max(ordinal) is wrong (picks the gloss-head, not the surface-final);
- every consumer (the genitive-prior derivation in wyrd-aicu.9, the grader, any future decomposition trainer) must work around it (cluster-grouped, order-robust) instead of trusting the sequence.

THE FIX: ordinals should encode SURFACE order (the order the elements appear left-to-right in the modern name). Approaches, roughly increasing effort:
(a) capture surface order at mining/ingest going forward (fix the parser prompt/extractor so the emitted element order is surface order);
(b) backfill from toponym_etymology_element.surface_in_modern where present — BUT it is NULL for ~395/403 (the scholars record the etymon, not which chars it covers), so coverage is thin;
(c) a re-ordering enrichment pass that aligns each element's reflex-surface to its position in the modern name and re-numbers ordinals by surface position (needs an element→span alignment; partly derivable via the matcher — but careful not to make the ground truth depend on the very decomposer it grades).
Recommend (a) for new mining + (c) as a deterministic backfill pass with a confidence flag, leaving genuinely-unalignable rows marked.

RELATED: wyrd-aicu.9 (the decomposer/genitive work that surfaced this; it currently works around the bug with cognate-cluster-grouped SET agreement). Until fixed, exact-sequence grading is not trustworthy. Investigation evidence: scratch/handoff-aicu9-*.md + scratch/aicu9_*probe.py (cross-region scan showed the head-first pattern).

Replaces wyrd-v2gm.18. Define register_effects.yaml catalog and composition semantics.

CATALOG SCHEMA:
Each register effect = triple of (phonological_feature_weights, semantic_tag_weights, position_bias). E.g.:

  harsh:
    phonological: {cluster_density: +0.6, final_fortition: +0.5, vowel_final_bias: -0.4}
    semantic_tags: {}
    position_bias: {}

  grim:
    phonological: {}
    semantic_tags: {death: +0.7, monster: +0.5, undead: +0.6, magic: +0.4, military: +0.3}
    position_bias: {}

  pastoral:
    phonological: {vowel_final_bias: +0.2, soft_consonants: +0.3}
    semantic_tags: {plant: +0.5, water: +0.5, agriculture: +0.6, tree: +0.4, bird: +0.4}

INITIAL ENTRIES (migration from MOODS): grim, harsh, pastoral, devotional, mortuary. Plus new entries the design discussion surfaced: noble, mystical, melodic, sinister, ancient, exotic, martial.

COMPOSITION RULES:
- Multiple effects sum component-wise (phonological vectors add, semantic tag weights add, position biases add)
- Clamp final composed vector to [-1, 1] per dimension
- Colon-suffix graduation (e.g. 'harsh:0.5') multiplies the effect's contribution by 0.5
- Order-independent (sum is commutative)

OPERATOR PATH FOR CUSTOM EFFECTS: edit register_effects.yaml; no code changes required.

ACCEPTANCE: catalog YAML round-trips through Python dataclass; existing 5 moods' compositions produce vectors that, when applied to corpus etymons via Phase A vectors, select the same name distributions the old MOODS dict produced (within seed-stable tolerance).

